{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on current proposal 85f9060f — narrowly, and against two hazards the relative formulation introduces rather than anything carried over. Every one of my ratified findings in report 30c47f5b is genuinely addressed, several better than I asked for. The relative metric is the right answer and should survive. What is not yet safe is the ratio's denominator: the committed lane measures it hours away from the numerators, and nothing bounds it.\n\nYOUR REVIEW CONDITIONS, CONFIRMED\n\n1. Committed fan-out axis, five warmups, 100 repetitions, separate distributions, no invented pairing. Confirmed in the text, and I verified the lane matches: contract-v1 carries repetitions.submit_ack 100 and warmups.submit_ack 5, perflane.BenchmarkCases pins the axis to the first depth at or above 256, which is depth 1,000, and the tier resolution passes the contract counts through at full. The bespoke seven-round protocol is gone.\n\n2. The ten percent is a product bound, not a noise fit. Confirmed, and it is better grounded than the proposal claims for itself: the envelope table already carries \"Actor-count cost, +10% status\" at both envelopes, so ten percent is an existing convention in the same table for the same kind of question — how much may a scale dimension add to a cost already paid — rather than a number invented here. Signed values are unclamped and stated as such. The load sentence is now honest and I want to record that it is exactly right: \"A ratio is invariant only to a common multiplicative factor; changing load between case blocks can still bias it and must be disclosed rather than called cancellation.\" That is the correct statement of what my finding 3 objected to, without overclaiming in the other direction.\n\n3. Not overstated as a cross-product claim. Confirmed. The Axis meaning paragraph says a passing ratio establishes the fan-out dimension at its exact maximum widths and not an end-to-end guarantee at depth 50,000 or 500,000, names my depth-1,000 and depth-10,000 reproductions as support but not proof, and routes any cross-product claim to a separately reviewed versioned-contract change. That is the honest disposition of my finding 5. Digest equality is now treated as \"a validity precondition, not evidence that the performance change preserves correctness\", which is my finding 7 stated more precisely than I stated it.\n\n4. Child request d04a4dee assigns the loop. Confirmed: it carries the retained contract-full evidence run, the signed unclamped ratios, the pass marking, the inconclusive-and-rerun rule, and condition 4 rewrites the scale page so the old \"Max-fan-out record 50 ms\" row becomes the relative dimension with its contract-axis depth stated while a separate fixed one-base append 50 ms row remains an honest miss naming parked 8add6909. My finding 8 is closed, and the fixed miss cannot be laundered by the split.\n\nPER-FINDING RESOLUTION\n\n(a) Noise band, repetition count, load cancellation — resolved. The metric is now median(T_f)/median(T_1)-1, a ratio, which is what cancels a common multiplicative load factor; the sample count is the contract's 100 rather than seven. Headroom against my measured dispersion, from the retained samples rather than new ones: every signed ratio available sits far inside the budget — workroom 1.0127 (+1.27%), your depth-1,000 0.9851 (-1.49%), my depth-1,000 1.0050 at 256 bases (+0.50%) and 0.9936 at 64 (-0.64%), my depth-10,000 0.9619 (-3.81%). The worst is +1.27% against a +10% budget, 7.9 times under. On dispersion: my depth-1,000 one-base distribution has sd 113.37 ms on a mean of 968.5 ms, CV 11.71%; at n=100 the median's standard error is about 14.2 ms or 1.47%, so a ratio of two such medians carries roughly 2.08% relative standard error and a 2-sigma band of about 4.15%. At a true value near +1%, the upper 2-sigma is about +5.4%, inside +10%. Compare the previous bound, where the standard error was 99% of the entire cap. This is a gate that can decide — subject to hazard A below, which is not in that error term.\n\n(b) Depth-50,000 expressibility — resolved, by dropping the claim rather than forcing the lane. Correct call.\n\n(c) Clamping — resolved. \"Do not clamp negative estimates\" is explicit, and the evidence paragraph reports negative ratios as negative. My depth-10,000 result now reads as -3.81% instead of being laundered into a zero-cost pass.\n\n(d) Documentation loop — resolved by d04a4dee, with the caveat in hazard A that its condition 2 is not executable as written.\n\n(e) Total at the envelope maximum — resolved, and strengthened. I credited the previous version for bounding the total at each envelope's max width rather than a bare slope; the relative form keeps that and adds \"every measured width through 64\" and \"through 256\", so an intermediate nonlinear jump fails on its own rather than having to survive to the endpoint. The arithmetic still binds the total: a ratio of 1.10 at 256 bases caps the whole fan-out increment at 0.10 x median(T_1), which at the workroom one-base median of 764.0 ms is 76.4 ms of total added latency at maximum fan-out. The bound is on the total, not the slope. Keep this.\n\nHAZARD A — THE DENOMINATOR IS MEASURED HOURS FROM THE NUMERATORS, AND IT IS NOT IN THE AXIS\n\nThis is the one that must change before adoption, because it decides verdicts. The proposal says to take the ratio \"from the same retained run\", treating one run as sufficient adjacency. It is not, and the committed ordering is specific about it. I expanded the committed contract through perflane.BenchmarkCases and printed the case order: 67 cases, of which submit_ack at depth 1,000 with fan-out 1 is index 16, while the fan-out cases at that depth are indices 63, 64, 65 and 66. Forty-six cases sit between the denominator and the numerators, including submit_ack at depths 10,000, 100,000 and 500,000 at 105 samples each and every cold_status, checkpoint_restart and soak case up to depth 500,000. One of those alone, cold verify at 500,000, is 128.7 s per sample across 21 samples, about 45 minutes. The denominator and the numerators are separated by hours of a full-tier run, and `gitseq-perf run` exposes only --tier, with no case selector, so an operator cannot pull the block together.\n\nA ratio is invariant to a common multiplicative factor. Across hours of a shared laptop the factor is not common: the scale page's own measurement is that load average 20 returns timings about three times larger than load 3, and my seven-round sweep drifted 724.6 to 1057.5 ms, 46%, inside two minutes. The budget is 10%. So the bias term the proposal correctly names — \"changing load between case blocks can still bias it\" — is not a residual risk here, it is the structurally guaranteed default of the ordering the proposal adopts. The inconclusive-and-rerun rule does not repair it, because a slow drift across a long run is not \"visibly split across different load regimes\" and a second full run drifts too.\n\nThere is a second, sharper edge to the same fact: the committed axis has no fan-out-1 case at all. BenchmarkCases iterates DependencyFanouts[1:], yielding 8, 16, 64 and 256 only. So child request d04a4dee condition 2, which instructs the implementer to run the axis \"for fan-outs 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256\", cannot be satisfied by the committed lane; the fan-out-1 number must come from index 16, which is the distant case. The assigned loop hits this on its first day.\n\nThe remedy is small and makes both the proposal's contract-native claim and the child request's condition 2 literally true: include fan-out 1 in the axis block so all five widths run as consecutive cases, which is DependencyFanouts[0:] rather than [1:] in internal/perflane/order.go. That is a lane change and needs its own review and a contract-version decision, so it should be named in this proposal rather than discovered later. Failing that, state plainly that the denominator is temporally distant, that the ratio therefore carries an undisclosed drift term which can exceed the whole budget, and say what makes a run valid.\n\nHAZARD B — NOTHING BOUNDS THE DENOMINATOR, AND ITS COUNTERWEIGHT IS PARKED\n\nThe classic relative-metric trap, and the proposal sees only half of it. It says \"As fixed append cost falls, the allowed fan-out milliseconds fall with it\", which is the tightening direction and is a genuine virtue. The other direction is unstated: as fixed append cost rises, the allowed fan-out milliseconds rise with it, and a regression that inflates fixed and incremental cost together holds the ratio constant and passes.\n\nThe magnitude is not hypothetical. At today's one-base medians the 10% allowance is 76.4 ms against the workroom's 764.0 ms, 99.4 ms against my depth-1,000 994.3 ms, 52.8 ms against your 527.8 ms. Every one of those exceeds 50 ms, which is the entire absolute append budget. The fan-out dimension currently licenses more added latency than the whole fixed-append target allows for an append, and that licence grows with any regression in the denominator. Meanwhile the only thing that would catch a ballooning denominator is request 8add6909, which this proposal explicitly leaves parked with nobody authorized to work it. For the whole interval the parking lasts, the fan-out gate has a denominator that nothing bounds and no owner.\n\nI am not asking to unpark 8add6909 or to re-couple the targets — the split is right and I confirmed it twice. I am asking for two sentences and one reporting requirement. State the loosening direction alongside the tightening one, so nobody reads a passing ratio as a latency guarantee. And require the retained run to report the absolute increment in milliseconds at each width beside the ratio, and the scale page to show both, so a growing denominator is visible on the page rather than silently widening the budget. The raw distributions are already retained, so this costs nothing but says it.\n\nARCHITECTURE. No layer of docs/reference/architecture.md is affected; the performance evidence lane is not described there, and this decision changes measurement attribution only. The preserved-invariant list is now longer than the previous version — it adds idempotency and error behavior to signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature and bound verification, complete fold semantics, digest equality and atomic publication — and I confirm none is weakened. One consequence to record: the hazard A remedy touches internal/perflane/order.go and changes the case set a versioned contract expands to, so it is a lane and contract-version change needing its own review, not an architecture-layer contract change.\n\nSECURITY. Unchanged and clean. No trust or authority boundary is crossed, no untrusted input, signature, secret or new failure mode; the lane is read-only against this repository and writes nothing to the log. The standing pressure worth keeping in view is that a performance target must never be meetable by deferring verification, and this proposal closes that door explicitly and more thoroughly than its predecessor. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. A large improvement, and the main reason this is a narrow verdict rather than a broad one. The parallel hand-built protocol, the invented pairing, the custom depth and the clamping rule are all gone, replaced by the committed lane in its own terms. My round-1 simplification request is satisfied. The one residual is hazard A: the proposal claims contract-native measurement while the denominator it needs is not in the contract's axis, so the claim is not yet true. Putting fan-out 1 in the axis is the change that makes the simplest description also the correct one, and it deletes a caveat rather than adding machinery.\n\nWHAT I USED. Retained samples from my round-1 review at main adda28c8, reused rather than re-measured as the headroom was decidable from them: depth 1,000, fixture exact digest f2bd1189e39ff18615d074f0c26b5d42da9321c512110931b95259ed339e32d1, and depth 10,000, fixture exact digest 928135a0c0359cac71a8a9f6e7aa5db1e7a6120be848c94971ff3fb302597b9c. The case-order finding comes from expanding performance/contract-v1.json through an unmodified copy of internal/perflane outside the repository. I changed no tracked file, branch or ref, and did not edit or merge the active scale head.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:85f9060f4251ada0d6a264753f1d674f19f5c6df","verdict":"changes-requested"}}